# Hosted fallback on Azure AI Foundry - use ONLY if your local model will not run.
#
#   1. Copy this file to env/hosted.env
#   2. Replace the two <...> values with the endpoint and key from your workshop instructors
#      (or from your own Azure AI Foundry deployment - see "Deploying your own" below)
#   3. Carry on with the workshop exactly as everyone else does
#
# This file overrides the model settings in every step, so you never edit it again.
# env/hosted.env is git-ignored - never commit a key.
#
# The deployment serves Qwen3-1.7B, the same model tier everyone else runs locally, so no exercise
# puts you at a disadvantage. It speaks Chat Completions but not the Responses API, so it must use
# the openai-chat: prefix. (Step 2 shows what happens with openai-responses: - an HTTP 400.)
MODEL=openai-chat:Qwen3-1.7B
OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://<your-foundry-resource>.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/
OPENAI_API_KEY=<your-api-key>
# The hosted service may not accept a reasoning-effort parameter, so send none.
REASONING=default

# Deploying your own:
#   - Find Qwen3-1.7B in the Azure AI Foundry model catalog: https://ai.azure.com/catalog/models?search=qwen
#   - Deploy it: https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/foundry-models/how-to/deploy-foundry-models
#     Qwen is a partner model, so the first deployment asks you to accept an Azure Marketplace offer.
#   - MODEL is openai-chat:<your deployment name>; the key is on the deployment's details page.
#
# Any other OpenAI-compatible endpoint works too: change the three lines above to its model, URL and key.
